WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.869 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  once again people took the streets of  sudan  thousands rallied in cartoon  it's divine city om durman  and across the nile river in hartung  north  despite security forces closing bridges  and main roads and shutting down the  internet for nearly three weeks they are  demanding a return to the transition  process and a civilian-led government  the military takeover two weeks ago  dissolved the transitional government  and arrested senior governments  officials and political figures from the  forces of freedom and change coalition  we the sudanese people will not accept a  military rule we don't want any  negotiations or compromise we want  civilians only to rule our country  the civilian groups including the forces  of freedom and change have called on  people to attend the mass protests on  saturday and people have responded they  are demanding an immediate restoration  of the civilian government the release  of the political prisoners and for the  army to commit to the constitutional  declaration it's signed with civilian  leaders in 2019  unarmed protesters were met with tear  gas  and live ammunition  people have been killed people have been  imprisoned and tortured there's nothing  worse than death and we have seen our  brothers being killed in front of us so  we are not going back the head of the  army has to be held accountable  the political crisis deepened when the  head of the army general abdulfatal  burhan formed a new ruling sovereign  council on thursday  the 14-member council includes army  leaders and civilians from across sudan  but not from the forces of freedom and  change we can see that in the  appointments that have been made in the  sovereign council and several of the  civilian members who do not represent  not just the ffc that the sort of um  political party model but any of their  revolutionary bodies so no one from  youth groups no one from anybody who  took part in in the  revolution to unseat bashir so what  we're seeing is very much a continuation  of the old bashir regime and it's meant  to be representative it's meant to be  you know you're meant to have someone  from the north from the center and  someone from the east was yet to be um  announced but no one feels that this  body actually represents uh the people  of sudan and so there's a lot of anger  today about just to what extent the  military is and entrenching its rule at  the moment mediation enforced by the  united nations as well as several other  countries have failed to end sudan's  crisis  protesters say they will continue to  voice the rejection of the army they  have called for similar protests in the  coming days saying they will only stop  when civilians are in charge of the  government  resulter al-jazeera khartoum

